WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:06.353
This field report shows how three business domains were locked down in one afternoon, using only free tools.

2
00:00:06.703 --> 00:00:11.119
DMARC, CAA, MTA-STS, TLS reporting, and a few edge settings.

3
00:00:11.469 --> 00:00:15.565
Every name you will see is a documentation example, not a real system.

4
00:00:17.667 --> 00:00:18.861
Why should you care?

5
00:00:19.211 --> 00:00:26.870
Money: forged invoices and fake messages from executives cost businesses billions of dollars every year, according to the FBI.

6
00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:34.601
Delivery: Gmail, Yahoo and Outlook now expect DMARC from bulk senders, and mail without it is more likely to land in spam.

7
00:00:34.951 --> 00:00:38.578
Trust: one convincing fake sent in your name can cost you a client.

8
00:00:38.928 --> 00:00:41.424
And cost: everything in this video is free.

9
00:00:43.533 --> 00:00:44.877
Here is the problem.

10
00:00:45.227 --> 00:00:49.473
An attacker's server can send a message that claims to be from your billing department.

11
00:00:49.823 --> 00:00:54.025
Email was designed with no proof of sender. The From line is just text.

12
00:00:54.375 --> 00:00:57.789
Without a published policy, the receiving server has to guess.

13
00:00:58.139 --> 00:01:03.323
The goal is to tell every mail server exactly what to do with mail that is not really from you.

14
00:01:05.433 --> 00:01:07.695
Three DNS records share the job.

15
00:01:08.045 --> 00:01:10.455
SPF lists the servers allowed to send.

16
00:01:10.805 --> 00:01:13.877
DKIM puts a cryptographic signature on every message.

17
00:01:14.227 --> 00:01:19.966
DMARC adds the missing rule: the domain in the From line must match, and if it does not, here is what to do.

18
00:01:20.316 --> 00:01:24.369
Strict alignment means an exact match, with no look-alike subdomains.

19
00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:28.548
The audit found three different states.

20
00:01:28.898 --> 00:01:37.261
One domain was already protected. One only monitored, so forged mail was reported but still delivered. One had no DMARC record at all.

21
00:01:37.611 --> 00:01:40.917
DMARC has three levels: none, quarantine and reject.

22
00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:46.665
Both weak domains moved to quarantine today. Reject comes after a clean week of reports.

23
00:01:48.767 --> 00:01:50.729
This is the record that was added.

24
00:01:51.079 --> 00:01:54.578
p equals quarantine sends failing mail to the spam folder.

25
00:01:54.928 --> 00:01:59.621
The rua address receives daily reports from Google, Microsoft and other providers.

26
00:01:59.971 --> 00:02:02.915
And the strict alignment tags close the look-alike loophole.

27
00:02:05.033 --> 00:02:06.889
Never trust the dashboard alone.

28
00:02:07.239 --> 00:02:11.079
Ask a public resolver the same question the rest of the internet asks.

29
00:02:11.429 --> 00:02:14.181
Status zero, no error, means the name exists.

30
00:02:14.531 --> 00:02:19.395
AD true means the answer is DNSSEC verified: signed, and not tampered with.

31
00:02:19.745 --> 00:02:23.649
And the data line is the exact record every mail server now sees.

32
00:02:25.767 --> 00:02:26.812
Next, CAA.

33
00:02:27.162 --> 00:02:32.687
It is a short allow-list of the certificate authorities that may ever issue a certificate for this domain.

34
00:02:33.037 --> 00:02:39.587
Any authority not on the list must refuse, so a rogue or tricked authority cannot mint a valid certificate for your name.

35
00:02:39.937 --> 00:02:45.739
The list was copied from a domain whose automatic renewals already work, so nothing breaks at renewal time.

36
00:02:47.867 --> 00:02:51.728
Mail between servers is encrypted only if both sides agree to it.

37
00:02:52.078 --> 00:02:57.646
An attacker on the network path can quietly strip that offer, and the mail falls back to plain text.

38
00:02:57.996 --> 00:03:03.799
MTA-STS publishes a rule: deliver to me over encryption, to these exact hosts, or not at all.

39
00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:09.676
The policy is a four line text file served over HTTPS.

40
00:03:10.026 --> 00:03:14.357
A tiny edge worker serves it for all three domains, with no server to patch.

41
00:03:14.707 --> 00:03:21.853
It starts in testing mode: senders report problems but still deliver. Enforcement comes only after the reports are clean.

42
00:03:22.203 --> 00:03:27.195
Two TXT records announce the policy, and send TLS reports to a mailbox.

43
00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:31.156
Then four small edge switches.

44
00:03:31.506 --> 00:03:36.413
Zero round trip resumption was turned off, because resumed requests can be replayed.

45
00:03:36.763 --> 00:03:41.989
The browser integrity check was turned on everywhere. The managed rule set stays always on.

46
00:03:42.339 --> 00:03:47.843
Fourteen API tokens became two, because every unused key is a spare key to your domains.

47
00:03:48.193 --> 00:03:55.297
Each switch was tested right after the change: the websites, the music player, a media client and a link preview bot all still answered.

48
00:03:57.400 --> 00:03:59.213
Finally, prove nothing broke.

49
00:03:59.563 --> 00:04:03.809
The same audit script ran again, and every check passed on all three domains.

50
00:04:04.159 --> 00:04:10.601
Mail records were identical, the websites answered, and the change log shows exactly who changed what, and when.

51
00:04:12.733 --> 00:04:14.803
Free, verifiable and reversible.

52
00:04:15.153 --> 00:04:21.190
Quarantine now, reject later. Testing now, enforce later. And always verify from the outside.
